{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "72",
    "case_summary": "A 23-year-old white male with a 4-year history of Crohn's disease presented with a sudden onset of malaise, fever, abdominal pain, vomiting, and stomal diarrhea over two days. He reported joint pains in the shoulders, elbows, wrists, metacarpophalangeal joints, knees, and ankles. Additionally, he had a rash on the elbows, ankles, and feet, which started as red macules and progressed to vesico-pustular lesions followed by crusting. There was no history of sexual exposure or recent infections. He was not on any regular medication but had stopped taking Pentasa four months prior. One month earlier, he had a laparotomy to remove a complex ileo-cutaneous fistula with blind tracts, and two weeks earlier, a defunctioning ileostomy was performed due to ongoing abdominal pain. The cutaneous fistula had been present for a year and was associated with a terminal ileal stricture and ileo-rectal fistula for at least two years. His bowel disease had not responded to immunosuppressive drugs, including azathioprine, corticosteroids, and three infusions of Infliximab a year earlier. There had been no extra-intestinal symptoms. Upon hospital admission, he was thin, afebrile, with a resting heart rate of 125 beats per minute. The rest of the cardio-respiratory examination was normal. The abdomen was minimally tender around the ileostomy without guarding or rebound tenderness. Skin examination revealed pustules and crusts around the elbows, ankles, and feet, sparing the buttocks. Clinical signs of synovitis were observed in the wrists, proximal interphalangeal, and metacarpophalangeal joints, as well as both ankles. Laboratory tests showed a hemoglobin level of 13.1 gm/dl, white blood cell count of 15.8 × 10^9/L, platelet count of 585 × 10^9/L, C-reactive protein level of 37.7 mg/L, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 69 mm/hr. Urea and electrolytes, complement levels, urine analysis, and microscopy were normal. Tests for rheumatoid factor, antinuclear and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, cryoglobulins, and Hepatitis B and C serology were negative. A transthoracic echocardiogram showed no signs of endocarditis, and multiple blood cultures were sterile.\n\nA skin biopsy from the ankle revealed a perivascular lymphohistiocytic infiltrate with prominent neutrophils and associated fibrinoid necrosis of vessels consistent with 'leukocytoclastic' vasculitis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
